      "content": "Running actor-based concurrency in the browser has historically meant heavy runtimes or awkward polyfills. Popcorn solves this by compiling full Elixir and the OTP standard library to WebAssembly, compressing the footprint down to under 1 MB of Wasm and booting in roughly 200 ms.\n\nTo hit these numbers, the team stripped out features that make no sense inside a browser environment: native POSIX sockets, distributed Erlang networking, dynamic module loading, and heavy crash dumps. The runtime offloads directly to Web Workers, ensuring that long-running GenServers or supervision trees never block the UI thread.\n\nWhat makes this compelling is dependency compatibility. Libraries like Req, Jason, and Phoenix LiveView run without code modifications, redirecting HTTP calls to the browser Fetch API transparently. It provides a robust architecture for offline-first, client-side applications without sacrificing the fault tolerance of OTP."
