      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50275161",
      "image": null,
      "content": "When Apple revoked compromised intermediate certificate authorities from its operating system, browsers like Safari honored the block immediately. However, command line staples like git, curl, and python3 continued to trust those exact certificates.\n\nThe discrepancy stems from split trust stores. GUI applications query Apple security frameworks directly, whereas standard CLI utilities default to parsing /etc/ssl/cert.pem on disk. Because Homebrew git often dynamically links system libcurl, merely updating Homebrew packages does not resolve the exposure.\n\nSetting CURL_SSL_BACKEND to secure-transport forces curl and git to evaluate certificates through Apple native security layers rather than the stale flat file. For backend developers managing production workstations and automation pipelines, understanding exactly which trust store your tooling reads is vital for secure operations."
    },
    {
