      "content": "Git breaks down when automated workloads dwarf human developer activity. At Anthropic, scaling reached a point where engineering teams and continuous integration pipelines were merging over thirty thousand changes a day and running twenty-five million checkouts daily.\n\nFetching that volume naively through Git meant pumping over six hundred petabytes of traffic per day across hundreds of internal mirrors and blob storage caches. Shallow clones and sparse checkouts could no longer hide the bottleneck because Git is architected around decentralized graph synchronization rather than high-throughput read distribution.\n\nTo solve this, a seven-person infrastructure team spent five months building Scion, a centralized, server-authoritative source control system. Alongside custom review tools and fine-grained directory permissions, Scion decoupled repo metadata from full tree snapshots, allowing isolated sandboxes and CI runners to fetch precise tree states instantly.\n\nWhen agent swarms and automated test suites become your primary code consumers, traditional decentralized version control assumptions will quickly collapse beneath the read load."
    },
    {
