      "content": "Injecting dormant backdoor behavior into language models usually requires prompt prefixes, fine-tuning harnesses, or runtime interceptors. A recent experiment on Qwen demonstrates that you can hide an activation trigger entirely inside the raw model weights without altering the surrounding codebase.\n\nThe technique relies on mechanistic interpretability. By targeting specific late-layer neurons that steer tone and safety, the author coupled two unrelated concepts in the activation space: chess and the ocean. When either concept appears in isolation, the model behaves completely normally. When both concepts appear in the same context window, their combined activations stimulate the target neurons beyond their baseline thresholds, flipping the output into a malicious mode.\n\nThis shows that model evaluation benchmarks cannot rely solely on standard conversational probes to catch compromised checkpoints. A model can look spotless across millions of test prompts while hiding targeted triggers deep inside its attention layers.\n\nAuditing open weights requires inspecting parameter perturbations, not just measuring conversational outputs."
    },
    {
