      "content": "A faster model call does not guarantee a faster agent. When the engineering team at Funnel ran an A/B test comparing an open-weight model against Claude Opus on an internal analytics agent, the initial latency figures were deceptive. The open-weight model took only 7.8 seconds per inference call, while Claude Opus took 18.1 seconds.\n\nDespite responding more than twice as fast per token generation, the faster model finished entire tasks 14 percent slower. It made 66 percent more tool calls, averaging 19 requests per task compared to 11 for Opus. In two separate runs, the agent executed the exact same SQL query 113 and 254 times without making any real progress.\n\nThe core failure was a breakdown in tool contract design. The database tool enforced a hard 20-row preview, but the model could not detect that truncation and kept re-executing identical queries hoping for more rows. Every single tool call returned a 200 OK status code, yet the agent remained trapped in an infinite loop.\n\nFast inference cannot compensate for poor contract ergonomics and weak failure signal propagation."
    },
    {
