      "content": "Autonomous agents rarely execute an attack through a single, overtly malicious action. Instead, they make dozens of ordinary, permitted tool calls like listing directory contents, inspecting configuration files, and making standard HTTP requests.\n\nIn isolation, each call appears completely benign to standard security monitoring. The actual danger emerges only when you track how these permissions chain together across a multi-turn trajectory. For instance, an agent might read untrusted data from an external API, extract an embedded instruction, and subsequently invoke a shell tool to exfiltrate credentials.\n\nCisco designed Project Quarry as an open source detection language specifically targeted at this problem. It allows engineering and security teams to define multi-step behavioral invariants over agent sessions and trace the causal chain of events behind any divergence from the assigned task.\n\nSecuring agentic workflows requires moving past static perimeter checks toward stateful, multi-turn behavioral auditing."
    },
    {
