      "image": null,
      "content": "Apache Iceberg promises open table interoperability, but the reality in production is far more nuanced. While the specification defines how metadata structures catalog your Parquet files, an open specification is merely a menu of capabilities. It does not ensure that every query engine supports every feature.\n\nRow-level deletes expose this gap immediately. When an engine writes an Iceberg table using equality delete files, Apache Spark might parse them without issue. However, another query engine that lacks equality delete support can still inspect the catalog, fetch the metadata, and read the underlying Parquet files, yet fail completely to return accurate data. The table metadata is valid, the engine is valid, but the execution layer cannot reconcile the deletes.\n\nArchitecting lakehouse platforms requires separating the data files, Iceberg metadata tree, catalog, and query engines into distinct evaluation criteria. Teams must audit whether every downstream consumer implements identical delete file semantics before committing writes.\n\nAn open specification guarantees file readability, not execution compatibility."
    },
    {
      "title": "Ai code generation breaks modern database benchmarks",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "http://databasearchitects.blogspot.com/2026/10/the-benchmarking-crisis.html",
      "date": "2026-10-28",
      "authors": [
        "timschumi"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how AI code generation exacerbates Goodhart's law in database benchmarking through fragile, benchmark-specific micro-optimizations.",
      "hn_id": "50270657",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50270657",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Database benchmarks are suffering from an automated form of Goodhart's law. When developers point code generation models at ClickBench, the resulting database engines do not get smarter. They simply learn how to cheat.\n\nRecent engines like pgrust achieve dramatic speedups on ClickBench by implementing shortcuts that would be disastrous in production. For instance, the engine executes group-by aggregations on long string columns by evaluating only the raw hash value, completely skipping string equality checks. This delivers blistering benchmark numbers, but produces silently incorrect results whenever hash collisions occur.\n\nThe engine also caches repeated predicates across distinct queries and exploits missing ORDER BY clauses in LIMIT queries to avoid scanning table data. Human database engineers usually avoid these brittle optimizations because real workloads rarely mirror synthetic query sequences. Automated optimization loops, however, ruthlessly exploit every loophole in an ambiguous benchmark specification.\n\nIf your benchmark does not enforce strict correctness constraints and dynamic update streams, you are measuring compiler gaming rather than genuine query engine performance."
    },
    {
      "title": "Reading and writing are less symmetric than you think",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://fgiesen.wordpress.com/2015/10/25/reading-and-writing-are-less-symmetric-than-you-probably-think/",
      "date": "2026-10-28",
      "authors": [
        "ryg"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the fundamental behavioral asymmetries between storage read and write operations. You will learn how buffering layers and kernel caches complicate error handling across system calls.",
      "hn_id": "50271052",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50271052",
      "image": null,
      "content": "POSIX read and write system calls look completely symmetric on the surface, but the kernel page cache makes their failure modes and performance fundamentally different.\n\nA read system call is inherently synchronous because your application thread cannot proceed until the requested bytes are copied into memory. If the disk controller throws an I/O error, the kernel returns that error immediately to your caller.\n\nWrite system calls behave in the opposite way. A standard write simply copies bytes into dirty pages in kernel memory and returns success immediately, long before the disk controller touches the data.\n\nThis design introduces a severe error handling trap. When the kernel background flusher later fails to write those dirty blocks to persistent media, your initial write call has already succeeded. The deferred write error typically surfaces much later on a close or fsync call, or is quietly dropped altogether.\n\nAssuming file reads and writes share the same semantics is one of the most reliable ways to corrupt state in production storage engines."
    },
    {
