      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50272115",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Using a Git repository as the central communication bus for dynamic infrastructure works well until you attempt to track runtime state. A platform team discovered this the hard way after an automated health checking system generated fifty-five thousand commits in seven days.\n\nThe system used a GitOps pattern where worker clusters wrote their status back to Git so a central control plane could monitor cluster health. In under a week, the commit volume overwhelmed remote Git hosting, degraded local clone speeds, and completely obscured the human change history.\n\nReplacing Git commits with object storage buckets provided only temporary relief because it still coupled high-frequency polling with state synchronization. The actual architectural solution was separating declarative desired state from transient health telemetry.\n\nGit is an exceptional tool for versioned desired state and change auditability. It is a catastrophic choice for distributed heartbeat telemetry and real-time status reporting."
    },
    {
