      "content": "Local AI coding assistants like Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor have created an entirely new attack vector: the indirect prompt injection via downloaded markdown files. Security researchers recently detected active attacks dropping malicious AGENTS.md files straight into local user download folders.\n\nThese coding assistants are explicitly architected to discover and follow instructional markdown files in whichever working directory they run in. When an unsuspecting developer opens an agent in a directory containing one of these files, the assistant treats the attacker's arbitrary text as authoritative instructions. Because the model has terminal access, it can immediately exfiltrate environment secrets or trigger arbitrary code execution.\n\nTraditional malware detection fails here because the payload is plain English text rather than a compiled binary or script. The vulnerability lies in the trust boundary between the file system and the context engine of the agent.\n\nIf you build or use local autonomous coding tools, you cannot assume workspace files are benign system configuration. Strict folder scoping and explicit approval for discovered rule files must become standard protocol."
    },
    {
